# declaro-persistum, declared one function at a time.
#
# SOURCES, IN ORDER, AND NOTHING ELSE. `HOW-IT-SHOULD-WORK.md`, then
# `docs/architecture.md`, then `tests/bdd/features/`. No source file is opened
# to write this: a declaration written from the code describes what exists, and
# then the two are one thing wearing two names and neither can check the other.
#
# BUILT ONE CARD AT A TIME, EACH READ ON THE MAP BEFORE THE NEXT. Nothing is here
# that was not argued for. The last attempt was a whole board written at once and
# defended for eleven rounds; this one gets read before it grows.

module declaro-persistum

  layer data

  # `Database` IS BACK, AND EVERY FIELD NOW HAS A LINE BEHIND IT. It was cut for
  # being assembled out of signatures I had just written. It returns because
  # `session_connect` is the function whose whole job is to produce it.
  #
  # A PURE FUNCTION USED TO BUILD IT AND CANNOT. `assemble` took the driver's
  # connection as an argument, and naming a handle in a signature is reading it.
  # Holding one inside a record is not, so `session_connect` puts the four fields
  # together itself. That is what an orchestrator is: the thing that keeps what
  # each step returned.
  #
  #   engine      `tests/bdd/features/opening/session_open.feature`: the
  #               database carries the engine, and that is the only place it is
  #               written down.
  #   connection  a replicated database connects at open and holds it for life.
  #               `query_run_statement` has nothing to run on without it.
  #   shutdown    `architecture.md:314`: required at open, and `lifecycle_shut_down` is
  #               the function that reads it.
  #   primary     `tests/bdd/features/database/opening.feature:14`: whether a
  #               database replicates is DERIVED from the primary rather than
  #               stored beside it, because a second flag could disagree.
  # A FAULT SAYS WHAT TO DO DIFFERENTLY, AND THAT IS WHY IT HAS TWO FIELDS.
  # Naming what went wrong is half a fault. The other half is the edit the
  # programmer should make instead, which is the difference between "no such
  # column" from an engine and "Person declares id and name, and you query_ask for
  # nmae". A fault with one field would let the second half be forgotten.
  #
  # IT TRAVELS BY RETURN AND BY NOTHING ELSE. Every function below that can fail
  # says so in its return type, and so does every caller of one, out to the door
  # the caller knocked on. No log line, no exception thrown past the signatures.
  type Fault = { kind: FaultKind
                 what_went_wrong: str
                 what_to_do_instead: str } note "two fields, because naming the problem is only half of one"

  # WHETHER TO TRY AGAIN IS ASKED OF THE KIND, NEVER OF THE MESSAGE.
  # `tests/bdd/features/running/retrying_is_the_librarys_job.feature`: a conflict
  # is retried and a refusal is not, and a spent budget must be told apart from a
  # refusal. `cut_short` is the platform killing a shutdown before it finished.
  set fault_kinds = { "conflict" : "two writers wrote one row; try again", "refused" : "the request itself is wrong; change it", "budget_spent" : "retried as many times as allowed", "cut_short" : "the process was killed before the work finished" } note "the kind decides whether to try again"
  vocabulary FaultKind = { fault_kinds } note "a conflict is retried and a refusal is not"

  # A VALUE, AND THE SMALLEST ONE. `HOW-IT-SHOULD-WORK.md` says every string you
  # pass is either checked against your declaration or bound as a parameter.
  # This is the bound half, and it is the only place a caller's own data lives.
  type Value = str | int | float | bool | NOTHING note "the bound half of every string you pass"
  type Row = dict<str, Value> note "a column name to a value, and nothing else"
  type Rows = list<Row> note "what a driver hands back"

  # THE ONE VALUE WE DID NOT MAKE. A driver's
  # connection has a shape, and it belongs to the driver. `architecture.md:98`
  # refuses to work an engine out from one, and reading its interior is how that
  # starts. `handle` is the format's word for it as of today: held, passed to a
  # boundary, never read. A pure function that names one is now a fault.
  #
  # `Callables` WAS HERE AND IS GONE. `compile_statement` is the only card that
  # knows the engine, so a second bag of engine-specific functions on the handle
  # had nothing left to do.
  # `Asked` is the call as the caller phrased it. Its keys are column names and
  # the vocabulary's own words; its values are the six things below and nothing
  # else, so a call of bare integers is not one of them.
  type Connection = handle note "held and passed to a boundary, never opened"
  type AskedValue = Value | Row | Rows | Condition | list<str> | dict<str, Direction> note "the six things a caller may write beside a key"
  type Asked = dict<str, AskedValue> note "the call as the caller phrased it"

  type Database = { engine: Engine
                    connection: Connection
                    shutdown: Shutdown
                    primary: str | NOTHING } note "what opening gives you, carried everywhere afterwards"

  # WHAT A MODEL SAYS, WHICH IS THE ONLY DESCRIPTION OF THE DATABASE. A column
  # carries its facets, which is what `field` declares: a key, a floor, a
  # length. One of those facets is a foreign key, and that is the whole of what
  # a join needs. `HOW-IT-SHOULD-WORK.md`: you declared the relationship once,
  # in the model, and naming it again in every query is the duplication this
  # library exists to remove.
  type Model = { table: str
                 columns: list<Column>
                 references: dict<str, str> } note "your only description of the database"

  # --- the schema, which is the models and nothing else --------------------
  #
  # `HOW-IT-SHOULD-WORK.md`: you change a model, the library reads the live
  # database, compares it to your models, works out the difference and applies
  # it. The models are the desired state and everything else is derived.

  # A column's facets are what `field` declares: a key, a floor, a length. The
  # set of types a model may write is in
  # `schema-declared/every_column_type_is_declarable.feature`, which exists
  # because a type the loader cannot read is a model nobody can declare.
  type Column = { name: str
                  declared_type: str
                  facets: dict<str, Value> } note "a name, a declared type, and what field() said about it"

  type Index = { name: str
                 columns: list<str>
                 unique: bool } note "a promise about speed"

  # A CONSTRAINT IS A RULE ABOUT A COLUMN, AND IT IS DATA HERE FOR THE REASON
  # EVERY OTHER RULE IS. `HOW-IT-SHOULD-WORK.md`'s test catches
  # `field(check="total >= 0")` the same way it catches a WHERE clause given as
  # text: that string is neither a declared name nor a bound value, so the only
  # thing left to do with it is paste it in.
  type Constraint = { name: str
                      columns: list<str>
                      rule: Condition } note "a rule about a column, as data rather than a string"

  type Table = { name: str
                 columns: list<Column>
                 indexes: list<Index>
                 constraints: list<Constraint> } note "what one table declares, columns and promises together"

  # A VIEW IS A DECLARATION WITH A NAME, NOT A STRING. `a_view_is_a_named_
  # declaration.feature`: you declare it against your models and the library
  # writes the SELECT, which only means something if what is stored is the same
  # `Declaration` a caller asks with. A plain view has no freshness to state.
  type View = { name: str
                declaration: Declaration
                freshness: Freshness | NOTHING } note "a query with a name, holding a declaration rather than a SELECT"

  # HOW STALE YOU CAN STAND IT, IN THE LIBRARY'S WORDS. The three come from
  # `freshness_is_declared_in_plain_words.feature`, which refuses to let a
  # caller say `on_commit` or `trigger`: those are mechanisms, and picking one
  # is the library's job. A caller who has to know which engine they are on has
  # been handed that job back.
  set freshnesses = { "always current", "current when read", "refreshed when query_ask" } note "requirements, not mechanisms: no caller says on_commit"
  vocabulary Freshness = { freshnesses } note "how stale you can stand it, in plain words"

  type Schema = { tables: list<Table>
                  views: list<View> } note "every table and view, derived from the models"

  # WHAT THE DIFFER EMITS. The words are `schema/diff.feature`'s own. Each
  # operation says whether carrying it out destroys something, because
  # `losing_data_is_said_out_loud.feature` asks the library to say which of them
  # do, derived from the models rather than query_ask for.
  set operation_kinds = { "create_table", "drop_table", "add_column", "drop_column", "alter_column", "rename_column", "rename_table", "create_index", "drop_index", "create_view", "drop_view", "insert_enum_values" } note "every change the differ can emit"
  vocabulary OperationKind = { operation_kinds } note "what one change is"

  type Operation = { kind: OperationKind
                     table: str
                     detail: dict<str, Value>
                     destroys: bool } note "one change, and whether carrying it out destroys something"

  # An query_answer rather than an effect: `asking_what_would_change.feature` wants the
  # operations in the order they would run, and nothing to have changed.
  type Plan = { operations: list<Operation>
                destroys: list<str> } note "what would change, and which of it loses data"

  type Report = { plan: Plan
                  applied: int } note "what changed, against what was going to"

  # ENGINES DO NOT OFFER THE SAME THINGS, AND ONE TABLE SAYS SO.
  # `one_table_of_capabilities.feature`: PostgreSQL has a materialized view and
  # SQLite does not, so one is built from a table, its triggers and a registry
  # row. Split this across a function per operation and "native or built" ends
  # up encoded in function names, where nobody can read it.
  set features = { "materialized_view", "check_constraint", "partial_index", "enum", "range_type" } note "the things engines disagree about"
  vocabulary Feature = { features } note "what an engine may or may not do natively"

  type Capability = { native: bool
                      built_from: str } note "native, or what it is built from where it is not"

  # The three engines, and what in a target names each. Straight from the table
  # in `tests/bdd/features/opening/engine_of.feature`.
  set engines = { "sqlite" : "a bare path, or a sqlite scheme", "postgresql" : "a postgresql or postgres scheme", "turso" : "a libsql scheme, or a path with a primary" } note "three, and what in a target names each"
  vocabulary Engine = { engines } note "decided once, at open, from the target"

  # SPELLINGS MINE, MEANINGS NOT. The features say a shutdown policy must be
  # stated and that a local database promising to replicate is refused. Neither
  # writes down the two words, so rename these freely.
  set shutdowns = { "exit_immediately" : "stop, and anything only local is gone", "replicate_then_exit" : "push until the cloud has it, then stop" } note "no default, because there is no safe answer"
  vocabulary Shutdown = { shutdowns } note "what happens to your data when the process stops"

  # WHAT THE FOUR GRAMMARS PRODUCE, AND THE ONLY THING `compile_statement` READS.
  # `tests/bdd/features/rendering/render.feature` says why it exists: four
  # grammars into three engines is twelve conversions that have to agree, four
  # into one and one into three is seven. Every field below is a row in the
  # vocabulary tables of `HOW-IT-SHOULD-WORK.md`, nothing more.
  #
  # THE WRITE SIDE IS `wanted` AND `rows`. "Writing has the same shape", so
  # `add` and `change` are askings like `all` and `count`, and the rows they
  # carry sit in the last field. A read leaves it `NOTHING`.
  type Declaration = { model: str
                       wanted: Wanted
                       columns: list<str>
                       condition: Condition
                       order: dict<str, Direction>
                       grouping: list<str>
                       having: Condition
                       paging: dict<str, int> | NOTHING
                       rows: Rows | NOTHING
                       across: list<Join> | NOTHING } note "what four grammars produce and one compiler reads"

  # THE LIBRARY WORKS THE JOIN OUT, AND THIS IS WHAT IT WORKED OUT. Three ways
  # to reach the other side, all of them reading the same foreign key: the rows
  # that belong to a row, a condition on the far side, and a question used as a
  # value inside another question.
  type Join = { model: str
                by_column: str
                to_column: str
                condition: Condition } note "the join the library worked out from your foreign key"

  # THE WHOLE VOCABULARY, IN TWO LISTS, BECAUSE THE TWO HALVES TRAVEL
  # DIFFERENTLY. A read runs now and answers now. A write is deposited and
  # answered by a ticket, and the rows reach the engine later, on a spare
  # moment. Splitting the set is what lets the table below say so word by word.
  set readings = { "all", "one", "first", "page", "distinct", "exists", "count", "sum", "average", "smallest", "largest", "count_distinct" } note "twelve words that run now and answer now"
  set writings = { "add", "change", "change_all", "remove", "remove_all", "replace" } note "six words that are deposited and drained later"
  vocabulary Wanted = { readings, writings } note "the whole vocabulary, in two halves that travel differently"

  # Fifteen operators, named as keys because a condition is data.
  set operators = { "eq", "ne", "lt", "lte", "gt", "gte", "in", "not_in", "between", "like", "contains", "startswith", "endswith", "is_null", "is_not_null" } note "fifteen, named as keys because a condition is data"
  vocabulary Operator = { operators } note "the fifteen ways to narrow"

  # A BARE VALUE MEANS `eq`, AND THIS SHAPE DOES NOT SAY SO. Nor does it carry
  # `or` and `not`, which are keys holding a condition rather than a column. Two
  # known holes, left open rather than guessed at.
  type Comparison = dict<Operator, Value> note "an operator and the value bound to it"
  type Condition = dict<str, Comparison> note "data in every grammar, so it composes the same way everywhere"

  set directions = { "ascending", "descending" } note "keys break ties left to right"
  vocabulary Direction = { directions } note "ascending or descending, and nothing else"

  # THE KEY CAME WITH THE CALL, IT WAS NOT WORKED OUT.
  # `tests/bdd/features/grammars/the_grammar_is_recognized.feature` says the
  # shape IS the entry point, so which grammar spelled a question is known
  # before anything runs. It travels as a value and the table below reads it.
  set grammars = { "pseudo_sql", "django", "prisma", "sqlalchemy" } note "which entry point the caller reached for"
  vocabulary Grammar = { grammars } note "four spellings of one meaning"

  # THE TEXT AND THE VALUES KEPT OUT OF IT. `render.feature`: the statement is
  # rendered and the values are not. They come back beside it, untouched, and
  # the driver binds them. A value that reached the text would be a value nobody
  # bound, and no earlier check would have seen it happen.
  type Statement = { text: str
                     parameters: dict<str, Value> } note "the text, and the values kept out of it"

  # Rows, or a number when you query_ask how many, or the ticket a write leaves
  # with, or a named absence.
  # `architecture.md:120`: absence is `NOTHING`, not `None` and not an empty
  # result, because `None` is also what a nullable column holds.
  type Answer = Rows | int | Ticket | NOTHING note "rows, a number, a ticket, or a named absence"

  # THE WAITING ROOM, AND IT IS NOT A STORE. `architecture.md:140`: a room holds
  # the arrival order and the callers waiting on tickets, both empty whenever
  # nobody is mid-flight. A write waiting is a statement and its values, which is
  # the same pair `compile_statement` produces. The receipt a caller collects is
  # written out at `architecture.md:244` and is not here, because nothing on this
  # board collects one yet.
  type Ticket = str note "one caller's claim on one write"

  # WHAT A CALLER READS WHEN IT COMES BACK FOR ITS WRITE. `architecture.md:244`
  # writes the three fields out. `ok` false means one thing only: the engine
  # stayed busy for the whole retry budget. Anything refused outright never
  # becomes a receipt, it is raised, and telling those two apart is the caller's
  # whole reason to read this.
  type Receipt = { id: str
                   ok: bool
                   error: str } note "ok false means the engine stayed busy, and nothing else"
  type Room = { arrivals: list<Statement>
                waiting: list<Ticket> } note "a waiting room, not a store; empty when nobody is mid-flight"

  # `session_connect`'S OWN RECORD. The caller's three arguments on top, then what each
  # step returned, in the order the chain runs them.
  type Connecting = { target: str
                      shutdown: Shutdown
                      connections: int
                      engine: Engine
                      settings: dict<str, Value>
                      permitted: int
                      connection: Connection
                      database: Database } note "what session_connect holds while it works"

  # `lifecycle_shut_down`'S OWN RECORD. When it started and how many pushes it has made,
  # because every log line names the database and the time elapsed.
  type ShuttingDown = { databases: list<Database>
                        started_at: float
                        attempts: int } note "when it started and how many pushes it has made"

  # `lifecycle_catch_up`'S OWN RECORD. Whether a writer was waiting decides whether the
  # sync pass runs at all, so the query_answer is kept rather than query_ask twice.
  type CatchingUp = { db: Database
                      room: Room
                      writer_waiting: bool
                      attempts: int } note "whether a writer was waiting, asked once"

  # `schema_work_out`'S OWN RECORD.
  type Planning = { db: Database
                    models: list<Model>
                    live: Schema
                    target: Schema
                    plan: Plan } note "the live schema, the target, and the difference"

  # `schema_migrate`'S OWN RECORD. It carries the plan it is carrying out, so what ran
  # and what was going to run are the same list.
  type Migrating = { db: Database
                     models: list<Model>
                     plan: Plan
                     statements: list<Statement>
                     report: Report } note "the plan it is carrying out, so both lists are one"

  # `query_deliver`'S OWN RECORD.
  type Collecting = { room: Room
                      ticket: Ticket
                      receipt: Receipt } note "one ticket and what came back for it"

  # `query_answer`'S OWN RECORD, AND NOTHING ELSE NAMES IT. The four things the caller
  # said are the top half. The bottom half is what each step in the chain
  # produced, in the order the chain runs them, so the last field is what leaves
  # on the right.
  #
  # PRIVACY IS BY THE SIGNATURES, NOT BY A KEYWORD. Nothing in the format scopes
  # a type to a function. This one is private because `query_answer` is the only card
  # that names it, which is a thing you can check by reading, and the map shows
  # it: click any other function and this record does not appear.
  # NO FAULT FIELD HERE, DELIBERATELY. Each field holds what a step produced
  # when it worked. A step that fails returns its fault to `query_answer`, and `query_answer`
  # returns it onward, so a fault is never something you have to remember to
  # look at afterwards.
  type Answering = { db: Database
                     room: Room
                     model: Model
                     grammar: Grammar
                     query_ask: Asked
                     declaration: Declaration
                     statement: Statement
                     rows: Rows
                     query_answer: Answer } note "what the caller said, then what each step returned"

  # THE SAME QUESTION IS COMPILED ONCE. `running/a_statement_is_built_once.
  # feature`: the same declaration asked a thousand times is a thousand
  # identical statements, and building them is work nobody asked for. What is
  # held is the compiling, never the rows and never the values: each asking
  # binds its own, so a held statement can never answer with stale data.
  store compiled = compile_statement "a statement is built once; running/a_statement_is_built_once.feature"

  # --- column 1: input I/O, and there are three ways in -------------------
  #
  # A LIBRARY'S WORLD IS ITS CALLER. It has no file waiting to be read, so the
  # world arrives when somebody calls it, and the doors they call are the input
  # boundaries. That is what makes the four grammars boundaries rather than pure
  # functions, which is where I had them.

  # SESSION. A caller asks for a database. The target says which engine is behind
  # it, so nobody names one. `shutdown` and the bound on connections are stated
  # here because this is the only place they can be, and `shutdown` has no
  # default because there is no safe query_answer to whether your data survives.
  boundary_in fn session_open : (target: str, shutdown: Shutdown, connections: int) -> Database | Fault side_effect reads "network" invokes session_connect note "the target says which engine, so no caller ever names one"

  # QUERY. A caller asks a question, or declares a write. `HOW-IT-SHOULD-WORK.md`
  # names four ways to spell it and one meaning: pseudo-SQL, Django, Prisma and
  # SQLAlchemy, each a thin skin over the same declaration.
  #
  # ONE CARD HERE AND FOUR SPELLINGS, AND THAT IS NOT SETTLED. Four cards would
  # say the door is four doors; one says the four are spellings of it. What it
  # returns is left `any` until the function that consumes it is declared, which
  # is the next decision rather than this one.
  boundary_in fn query_ask : (db: Database, room: Room, model: Model, grammar: Grammar, query_ask: Asked) -> Answer | Fault side_effect reads "caller" invokes query_answer note "one door, four spellings; the grammar arrives with the call"

  # LIFECYCLE. The platform says stop, and nothing in the program query_ask for it. The
  # other two doors open because a caller called; this one opens whether or not
  # anything was ready.
  #
  # IT IS THE ONE WITH THE HIGHEST COST OF BEING WRONG. The usual deployment has
  # disk that does not survive the process, so anything unreplicated at exit is
  # gone, and the window between the signal and the kill is all there is.
  boundary_in fn lifecycle_stop : (registered: list<Database>) -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect reads "signal" invokes lifecycle_shut_down note "the platform says stop, and nothing asked it to"

  # LIFECYCLE. The runtime hands us a turn, and nothing outside query_ask for that
  # either. `architecture.md:227` is a loop that runs until stop is set and
  # calls `drain` on every pass, so a caller's write does not travel on the door
  # the caller knocked on. `room_deposit` hands back a ticket and returns; the write
  # leaves the process later, here.
  # THE CLOCK IS THE WORLD, AND READING IT IS INPUT. `engine_of.feature` says a
  # pure function reads no clock. This is the fourth role, added 2026-09-17: the
  # program asks, rather than the world calling in. It stands with the doors,
  # calls nothing, and heads no chain, and the three orchestrators that need the
  # time call it and hand its query_answer to the pure functions.
  reader fn clock_read : () -> float side_effect reads "clock" note "the program asks the clock; nothing arrives on its own"

  # SCHEMA. A caller asks what would change, and nothing changes.
  # `schema-declared/asking_what_would_change.feature`: nobody deploys a schema
  # change without reading it first, and no difference is an query_answer rather than
  # an empty effect.
  boundary_in fn schema_plan : (db: Database, models: list<Model>) -> Plan | Fault side_effect reads "caller" invokes schema_work_out note "an answer, not an effect: the database is left alone"

  # SCHEMA. A caller asks for the database to match the models. It writes no
  # migration script and never says "add a column"; reaching the declared state
  # is the library's problem.
  boundary_in fn schema_apply : (db: Database, models: list<Model>) -> Report | Fault side_effect reads "caller" invokes schema_migrate note "you declare the state; reaching it is the library's problem"

  # SCHEMA. A caller answers the one question the library refuses to guess.
  # `a_rename_is_asked_and_remembered.feature`: a column gone and another
  # appeared is either a rename or a drop and an add, and the difference is your
  # data. The query_answer is remembered, because asking again on every deploy asks in
  # the middle of a deploy, which is the worst moment and the one where people
  # say yes to anything.
  boundary_in fn schema_rename : (db: Database, gone: str, appeared: str) -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect reads "caller" invokes schema_remember note "the one question the library refuses to guess, answered once"

  # QUERY. A caller comes back for the write it deposited. `write_queue.feature`:
  # a ticket belongs to exactly one caller, so a failure reaches that caller and
  # nobody else, and a caller draws its own atomicity boundary by choosing when
  # to query_deliver. Deposit hands out the ticket; this is where it is spent.
  boundary_in fn query_collect : (room: Room, ticket: Ticket) -> Receipt | Fault side_effect reads "caller" invokes query_deliver note "the ticket a deposit handed out is spent here"

  # LIFECYCLE. The server starts. The one place the signal handler goes in.
  boundary_in fn lifecycle_start : () -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect reads "caller" invokes lifecycle_install note "the one place the signal handler goes in"

  # NO FLUSH DOOR. Decided 2026-09-17. `architecture.md:308`: no caller waits for
  # the primary, with exactly two exceptions, a cold open and shutdown, both at
  # the edges of the process. `replication.feature` has a scenario where a caller
  # awaits `flush`; that scenario contradicts the architecture and is the one to
  # change.

  boundary_in fn lifecycle_turn : (db: Database, room: Room) -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect reads "scheduler" invokes lifecycle_catch_up note "the runtime hands over a spare moment"

  # --- column 2: one orchestrator per door --------------------------------
  #
  # A DOOR RECEIVES AND AN ORCHESTRATOR SEQUENCES, and each door reaches exactly
  # one. What each of these invokes is the next decision rather than this one,
  # so none of them has an `invokes` yet: the pure core arrives when it is
  # declared, not before.

  # Works out which engine the target names, connects, copies the whole database
  # if this copy is cold, and hands back the handle everything downstream reads.
  orchestrator fn session_connect : (connecting: Connecting) -> Connecting | Fault invokes engine_of, refuse_unusable_settings, permitted_connections, session_open_connection, replicates, lifecycle_register, turso_pull, log_write note "works out the engine, connects, and keeps the handle"

  # Checks the columns against the model, builds the declaration, renders it for
  # this engine, runs it, and shapes what comes back.
  orchestrator fn query_answer : (asking: Answering) -> Answering | Fault invokes by_grammar, refuse_undeclared_columns, join_between, compile_statement, by_wanted, answer_from_rows, log_write side_effect reads_writes "store:compiled" note "checks the columns, compiles once, runs, shapes the answer"

  # Pushes until Turso has everything, retrying rather than giving up,
  # then closes. The one place blocking is correct.
  orchestrator fn lifecycle_shut_down : (shutting_down: ShuttingDown) -> ShuttingDown | Fault invokes replicates, clock_read, turso_push, next_wait, progress_line, log_write note "blocks until Turso has everything; the one place that is right"

  # Reads the live database, builds the target from the models, works out the
  # difference, refuses what is ambiguous and names what destroys data.
  orchestrator fn schema_work_out : (planning: Planning) -> Planning | Fault invokes refuse_owned_names, schema_from_models, schema_inspect, difference, name_what_is_ambiguous, order_operations, name_what_destroys, log_write note "reads the live database and says what would change"

  # THE SAME PLANNING, THEN CARRIES IT OUT. It reaches the same pure functions
  # in the same order as `schema_work_out`, so what a caller was shown and what runs
  # cannot come apart, and then spells each operation for this engine.
  orchestrator fn schema_migrate : (migrating: Migrating) -> Migrating | Fault invokes refuse_owned_names, schema_from_models, schema_inspect, difference, name_what_is_ambiguous, order_operations, name_what_destroys, capability_of, mechanism_for, spell_view, spell_operation, query_run_statement, log_write note "the same planning, then carries it out"

  # Writes the query_answer down where the next deploy will find it.
  orchestrator fn schema_remember : (db: Database, gone: str, appeared: str) -> NOTHING | Fault invokes schema_store_rename, log_write note "writes the answer where the next deploy will find it"

  # Waits for the drainer to finish this caller's write, hands back the receipt
  # and drops the ticket, so a collected ticket leaves the room empty again.
  orchestrator fn query_deliver : (query_collect: Collecting) -> Collecting | Fault invokes room_release_ticket, refusal_reaches_caller, log_write note "waits for the drainer, hands back the receipt, drops the ticket"

  # Empties the room one write at a time in arrival order, and, if nothing else
  # wants the connection, pushes and pulls on it.
  #
  # TWO PATHS AND ONE ORCHESTRATOR, DELIBERATELY. Draining and syncing turn on
  # the same question: is a writer waiting. `architecture.md:308` has
  # `replicate_if_idle` ask it, and the drainer is what knows the query_answer. Split
  # across two orchestrators, it gets query_ask twice and answered twice.
  orchestrator fn lifecycle_catch_up : (catching_up: CatchingUp) -> CatchingUp | Fault invokes writer_waiting, next_in_order, query_run_statement, worth_retrying, budget_spent, clock_read, next_wait, receipt_for, room_post_receipt, replicates, turso_push, turso_pull, log_write note "two uses of a free turn: drain the room, then sync if idle"

  # Installs the one signal handler. Safe to call again: the second call finds it
  # installed and returns.
  orchestrator fn lifecycle_install : () -> NOTHING | Fault invokes lifecycle_install_handler, log_write note "installing twice changes nothing"


  # --- column 3: output I/O, and there are four ways out ------------------
  #
  # AN OUTWARD CALL HANDS SOMETHING BACK, AND THAT RETURN IS NOT A DOOR. Rows
  # arrive because we query_ask the driver for them. The world does not push them at
  # us, so reading is not a fourth entrance.

  # SESSION. Ask the driver for a connection. `architecture.md:96` says the handle
  # carries its engine, decided once, so nothing downstream works it out again.
  boundary_out fn session_open_connection : (target: str, connections: int) -> Connection | Fault side_effect reads_writes "database" note "the driver hands back a handle we hold and never read"

  # QUERY. Run a statement on that connection. The driver does not know whether it
  # was a select or a create table, so this is one door rather than four.
  boundary_out fn query_run_statement : (db: Database, statement: Statement) -> Rows | int | Fault side_effect reads_writes "database" note "one door for every statement; the driver knows no difference"

  # THREE and FOUR. The sync channel, in its two directions. `architecture.md`
  # names exactly two moments where sync is on the critical path, one per
  # direction: a cold open copies the whole database before returning, and
  # shutdown pushes until Turso has everything. Two callers, two
  # directions, one connection. A card is a function, not a connection.
  boundary_out fn turso_pull : (db: Database) -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect reads "turso" note "the cold open blocks here, and nowhere else does a read wait"
  boundary_out fn turso_push : (db: Database) -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect writes "turso" note "shutdown blocks here, because unreplicated at exit is gone"

  # THE ODD ONE, AND IT RETURNS NOTHING. Every other outward call hands back a
  # value the caller uses. This one does not, which is why it is a door and not
  # a call. `architecture.md:114` keeps failures out of it: a failure comes back
  # as a value, never as a log line. `architecture.md:116` is why it can only be
  # reached from column 2: writing a log line is I/O, so a function in the
  # middle that logs has reached outside without changing its signature.
  # A WRITE LEAVES HERE AND THE CALLER DOES NOT WAIT. `architecture.md:201`: the
  # caller deposits and gets a ticket back immediately, and a crew of drainers
  # empties the queue afterwards. The room is not the outside world, but a value
  # put into it leaves this caller's hands and is picked up by another, which is
  # why this is a door rather than a step.
  boundary_out fn room_deposit : (room: Room, statement: Statement) -> Ticket | Fault side_effect writes "room" note "the caller leaves with a ticket and does not wait"

  # ONE HANDLER FOR THE PROCESS, INSTALLED AT STARTUP, AND INSTALLING IT TWICE
  # CHANGES NOTHING. Decided 2026-09-17: a handler per open database would stack
  # one on another, so it goes in once and a second call finds it there and
  # returns. `replication.feature`: any handler the host application already set
  # still runs. It chains; it never replaces.
  boundary_out fn lifecycle_install_handler : () -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect writes "signal" note "it chains to the handler already there; it never replaces one"

  # A DATABASE THAT ASKED TO REPLICATE AT EXIT PUTS ITSELF ON THE LIST THE ONE
  # HANDLER WORKS THROUGH. A database that did not ask stays off it, so a caller
  # who never opted in changes nothing about what happens at exit.
  boundary_out fn lifecycle_register : (db: Database) -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect writes "shutdown list" note "a database that asked to replicate puts itself on the list"

  # WAITS FOR THIS ONE TICKET AND TAKES IT OUT. The caller is parked here until
  # the drainer posts what happened, which is why a room_deposit could return at once.
  boundary_out fn room_release_ticket : (room: Room, ticket: Ticket) -> Receipt | Fault side_effect reads_writes "room" note "parks this caller until the drainer posts its outcome"

  # THE DRAINER PUTS THE OUTCOME WHERE THE WAITING CALLER WILL FIND IT. A
  # failure whose caller has already gone has nowhere to go, so the drainer logs
  # it, and that is the only place it can still be seen.
  boundary_out fn room_post_receipt : (room: Room, ticket: Ticket, receipt: Receipt) -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect writes "room" note "the drainer leaves the outcome where the caller will find it"

  # WHAT THE DATABASE ACTUALLY HOLDS. The program asks; nothing arrives on its
  # own, so this is a reader rather than a door. It is the only place the
  # library asks a database what is there, and it asks so it can compare, never
  # so it can decide what belongs.
  reader fn schema_inspect : (db: Database) -> Schema | Fault side_effect reads "database" note "the only place the library asks a database what it holds"

  # The remembered query_answer to a rename, kept in the library's own table so it
  # outlives the run that answered it.
  boundary_out fn schema_store_rename : (db: Database, gone: str, appeared: str) -> NOTHING | Fault side_effect writes "database" note "kept in the library's own table so it outlives the run"

  boundary_out fn log_write : (event: dict<str, Value>) -> NOTHING side_effect writes "log" note "an operator reads this afterwards; a failure never travels here"

  # CLOSING THE CONNECTION IS NOT HERE. The process ending closes it, and I
  # cannot yet say what we do that the operating system does not. It comes back
  # when something needs it.

  # --- the pure core, so far only what `session_connect` reaches -------------------

  # THE DECISION THE DOOR RESTS ON, AND THE SPECIFICATION ALREADY NAMES IT.
  # `tests/bdd/features/opening/engine_of.feature` is one feature for this one
  # function: a string in, one of three words out, or a refusal naming the
  # three. It opens nothing and reads no connection, which is the same refusal
  # `architecture.md:98` makes, and it is separate from the door because the
  # decision is the part worth testing without a database.
  fn engine_of : (target: str) -> Engine | Fault note "a string in, one of three words out, and it opens nothing"

  # A SETTING THAT MEANS NOTHING TO THE ENGINE IS REFUSED, NOT IGNORED. One door
  # accepts the union of what three engines need, so `primary` means nothing to
  # SQLite and a busy timeout means nothing to PostgreSQL. Ignoring one is a
  # silent default wearing a different hat.
  #
  # This is what is left of the shutdown check. Asking a database with no
  # primary to replicate at exit is one setting the engine cannot use, not a
  # rule of its own.
  fn refuse_unusable_settings : (engine: Engine, settings: dict<str, Value>) -> dict<str, Value> | Fault note "a setting this engine cannot use is refused, never ignored"

  # The caller's number is a request, not the query_answer: a crew is local-only
  # (`architecture.md:154`) and a second sync connection to a replica fails
  # (`architecture.md:179`). WHAT POSTGRESQL PERMITS IS NOT WRITTEN ANYWHERE I
  # HAVE READ, so a third of this table is missing.
  fn permitted_connections : (engine: Engine, connections: int) -> int | Fault note "the caller's number is a request; the engine decides"

  # --- what `query_answer` reaches ----------------------------------------------

  # THE LAST STEP, AND THE ONLY ONE THAT KNOWS WHICH ENGINE IT IS TALKING TO.
  # Everything before it is engine-free. Named `render` in
  # `tests/bdd/features/rendering/render.feature`; renamed here because the job
  # is compiling one representation into a target dialect, and because compiling
  # once and running many times with different values is what
  # `tests/bdd/features/running/a_statement_is_built_once.feature` describes.
  fn compile_statement : (declaration: Declaration, engine: Engine) -> Statement | Fault note "the only function that knows which engine it is talking to"

  # Every grammar refuses the same undeclared columns, in your own process. It
  # names your model and lists the columns it does declare, so no caller ever
  # sees "no such column" from an engine.
  # IT CHECKS AND IT DOES NOT PRODUCE. Returning the declaration it was handed
  # makes it look like a second source of declarations beside the four builders,
  # and the map said so: with two possible producers it refused to draw where a
  # `Declaration` comes from at all. A check answers whether something is wrong.
  fn refuse_undeclared_columns : (model: Model, declaration: Declaration) -> NOTHING | Fault note "you never see no such column from an engine"

  # Driver rows in, and out comes what was query_ask for: instances of the model, a
  # number, or `NOTHING`.
  fn answer_from_rows : (model: Model, rows: Rows, declaration: Declaration) -> Answer | Fault note "rows become instances of your model, a number, or NOTHING"

  # FOUR BUILDERS, ONE TABLE, AND NO GUESSING ANYWHERE.
  # The four cards below are what the four grammars produce, all of it the same
  # `Declaration`. `by_grammar` picks between them, and it is a lookup rather
  # than a detection: the key arrived with the call.
  #
  # WHERE EACH ASKING GOES, WORD BY WORD. Twelve run now; six are deposited and
  # drained later. Written out rather than reduced to a rule, because a reader
  # asking "does `exists` block" should read the query_answer rather than derive it.
  dispatch by_wanted = { "all" -> query_run_statement, "one" -> query_run_statement, "first" -> query_run_statement, "page" -> query_run_statement, "distinct" -> query_run_statement, "exists" -> query_run_statement, "count" -> query_run_statement, "sum" -> query_run_statement, "average" -> query_run_statement, "smallest" -> query_run_statement, "largest" -> query_run_statement, "count_distinct" -> query_run_statement, "add" -> room_deposit, "change" -> room_deposit, "change_all" -> room_deposit, "remove" -> room_deposit, "remove_all" -> room_deposit, "replace" -> room_deposit } note "twelve run now, six are deposited"

  # THIS IS WHAT KEEPS `query_ask` ONE DOOR. Four doors would say the caller entered
  # four different buildings. One door carrying which entry point was used says
  # the four are spellings, which is what `HOW-IT-SHOULD-WORK.md` says they are.
  dispatch by_grammar = { "pseudo_sql" -> declaration_from_pseudo_sql, "django" -> declaration_from_django, "prisma" -> declaration_from_prisma, "sqlalchemy" -> declaration_from_sqlalchemy } note "a lookup, not a detection: the key arrived with the call"

  fn declaration_from_pseudo_sql : (query_ask: Asked) -> Declaration | Fault note "SQL's word order without SQL's text"
  fn declaration_from_django : (query_ask: Asked) -> Declaration | Fault note "managers and lookups, for a team that already writes them"
  fn declaration_from_prisma : (query_ask: Asked) -> Declaration | Fault note "one call carrying named parts"
  fn declaration_from_sqlalchemy : (query_ask: Asked) -> Declaration | Fault note "a query object, for people who think in one"

  # --- what `lifecycle_shut_down` and `lifecycle_catch_up` reach ------------------------------

  # DERIVED, NEVER STORED. `database/opening.feature:14`: whether a database
  # replicates comes from its primary, because a second flag could disagree with
  # the first. It decides whether any sync door is reached at all.
  fn replicates : (db: Database) -> bool note "derived from the primary, because a second flag could disagree"

  # THE NEXT WAIT GROWS, AND TWO CALLERS DO NOT WAIT IN STEP. No random source:
  # the spread comes from the time and the write's ticket, so the same three
  # inputs always give the same wait and a test checks an exact number. The
  # ticket is what separates two callers who retry at the same instant, since
  # they read the same time.
  fn next_wait : (attempt: int, now: float, ticket: Ticket) -> float note "each wait is longer, and the ticket keeps two callers apart"

  # EVERY LINE NAMES THE DATABASE AND THE TIME ELAPSED, AND UNTIL THE PUSH ENDS
  # EACH ONE SAYS THE DATA HAS NOT YET REACHED THE CLOUD. `replication.feature`
  # asks that a shutdown cut short says so in its last line. Nothing runs after
  # the kill, so every line has to be fit to be the last one.
  fn progress_line : (db: Database, elapsed: float, done: bool) -> dict<str, Value> note "every line is fit to be the last one"

  # `architecture.md:308`: the sync pass is dropped when a writer is waiting.
  fn writer_waiting : (room: Room) -> bool note "a sync pass steps aside for a writer"

  # The room drains one write at a time, in arrival order.
  fn next_in_order : (room: Room) -> Statement | NOTHING note "the room drains one write at a time, in arrival order"

  # Asked of the fault's kind, never its message.
  fn worth_retrying : (fault: Fault) -> bool note "asked of the fault's kind, never of its message"

  # A bounded budget, and spending it is an query_answer of its own kind.
  fn budget_spent : (attempts: int, budget: int) -> bool note "a spent budget is an answer, not a refusal"

  # --- what `query_deliver` and the drainer reach -------------------------------

  # A REFUSAL IS RAISED, A SPENT BUDGET IS A RECEIPT. `write_queue.feature`: a
  # caller that cannot tell them apart will retry the one that can never succeed
  # and give up on the one that would have worked. Asked of the fault's kind.
  fn refusal_reaches_caller : (fault: Fault) -> bool note "a refusal is raised; a spent budget comes back as a receipt"

  # Builds what the caller will read out of what the engine did.
  fn receipt_for : (ticket: Ticket, failure: NOTHING | Fault) -> Receipt note "what the engine did, in the three fields a caller reads"

  # --- what the schema half reaches ---------------------------------------

  # THE LIBRARY KEEPS ITS OWN TABLES BESIDE YOURS, AND A COLLISION IS SILENT
  # UNTIL IT IS FATAL. `a_name_the_library_owns_is_refused.feature` lists the
  # prefixes: `_declaro_meta`, `_dp_`, `_declaro_tmp_`, and `sqlite_` and
  # `turso_`, which belong to the engines.
  fn refuse_owned_names : (models: list<Model>) -> NOTHING | Fault note "the library keeps its own tables beside yours"

  # The models are the desired state, so the target schema is derived from them
  # and from nothing the database says.
  fn schema_from_models : (models: list<Model>) -> Schema | Fault note "the models are the desired state; nothing else is asked"

  # Set theory over two schemas: what is only in the target is created, what is
  # only in the live database is dropped, what differs is altered.
  fn difference : (live: Schema, target: Schema) -> list<Operation> note "set theory over two schemas"

  # A COLUMN GONE AND ANOTHER APPEARED IS TWO DIFFERENT CHANGES. This stops and
  # says it cannot tell, unless the query_answer was remembered. Choosing wrong
  # destroys a column, so the fault it returns names the two columns and the
  # door that answers.
  fn name_what_is_ambiguous : (operations: list<Operation>, remembered: dict<str, str>) -> list<Operation> | Fault note "it stops rather than guessing, because guessing wrong costs a column"

  # Dependencies decide the order: a table before the index on it, a parent
  # before the child that points at it.
  fn order_operations : (operations: list<Operation>) -> list<Operation> | Fault note "a table before the index on it, a parent before its child"

  # Dropping a column loses what was in it, dropping a table loses all of it,
  # and narrowing a type loses what no longer fits.
  fn name_what_destroys : (operations: list<Operation>) -> Plan note "derived from your models, and said out loud"

  # Native here, built there, and the table says which.
  fn capability_of : (engine: Engine, feature: Feature) -> Capability note "native here, built there, and one table says which"

  # One operation into the statements that carry it out on this engine. The
  # schema half's `compile_statement`.
  fn spell_operation : (operation: Operation, engine: Engine) -> list<Statement> | Fault note "one operation into the statements that carry it out"

  # --- what a question across models reaches -------------------------------

  # THE FOREIGN KEY ALREADY SAID IT. Given the two models and the far-side
  # condition a caller wrote, this works out the join rather than asking the
  # caller to write it. A pair of models with no key between them is a fault
  # naming both, since no join exists to work out.
  fn join_between : (near: Model, far: Model, condition: Condition) -> Join | Fault note "the foreign key already said it; naming it again is the duplication"

  # --- what a declared view reaches ---------------------------------------

  # The library writes the SELECT, which is this: the same compiler the data
  # half uses, over the declaration the view holds.
  fn spell_view : (view: View, engine: Engine) -> Statement | Fault note "the library writes the SELECT you never wrote"

  # PICKS THE MECHANISM THE CALLER REFUSED TO NAME. PostgreSQL has a real
  # materialized view and SQLite does not, so one engine gets a declaration and
  # the other gets a table, its triggers and a registry row. Which is which is
  # in the capability table, not in a function name.
  fn mechanism_for : (freshness: Freshness, engine: Engine, capability: Capability) -> list<Operation> | Fault note "you say how fresh; picking the mechanism is the library's job"
